# Every stage directory is a Docker build context and every stage Dockerfile
# copies the whole of it into the image, so anything left lying about here gets
# baked in.  Nothing below is a file any stage needs: it is the debris that
# accumulates from running the suite locally, plus editor backups.
#
# This file is byte-identical in all three stage directories, and that is a
# property worth keeping rather than a coincidence.  A stage that excluded
# something the others kept would produce an image nobody could reproduce from
# the tree, and the difference would show up as one stage failing on a host where
# the other two passed.  The prose is therefore stage-neutral: it describes the
# debris, not which stage happens to produce it.
#
# Every pattern carries a `**/` prefix, and that is not decoration.  Docker
# matches a .dockerignore pattern against the whole context-relative path and
# does *not* re-apply it at each directory level the way .gitignore does, so a
# bare `__pycache__/` excludes a __pycache__ at the context root and nothing else
# -- not lib/__pycache__, which is the one place these suites reliably produce
# one, because every module imports from lib/ and an author who runs a driver by
# hand creates it on the first run.  PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE in a Dockerfile does
# not cover it either: that stops the *image* writing bytecode and has no effect
# on COPY bringing the author's bytecode in.  The two failures are
# indistinguishable from inside the Dockerfile and differ only by the interpreter
# tag on the filename -- an author's host is 3.10, the images are 3.11, and a
# stale 3.10 .pyc shadows nothing but is still shipped.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/*~
**/*.orig
**/*.rej
**/.DS_Store

# Node debris.  data/buildtools-package.json is a real `npm ci` input in two of
# the three stages, and an author who ran that install here to check a rollup
# version by hand would ship tens of megabytes of bundler beside it.
**/node_modules
**/package-lock.json.orig

# Rust and build debris.  No crate sources live in any of these contexts, but the
# suites build submissions and references into work directories, and an author
# pointing --work at the tree instead of /tmp leaves both behind.  `target/` would
# be a prebuilt binary in an image whose whole job is to build one from submitted
# source.
**/target
**/*.rlib
**/work
**/logs
